SSD vs HDD
a. HDD (Hard Disk Drive)
Mechanical disk. Moving parts.
Think of it like a record player.
Traditional storage device with moving mechanical parts:
- Spinning magnetic platters
- Read/write head
- Motor

Pros:
- Cheaper per TB
- Large capacities (10TB, 20TB+)

Cons:
- Slower
- Mechanical failure possible
- Higher power consumption

Typical Uses:
- Backups
- Archival storage
- Cold data

b. SSD (Solid-State Drive)
Flash memory.
Uses flash memory chips with no moving parts.

Think of it like a giant, very fast USB drive.

Pros:
- Much faster
- Lower latency
- More durable
- Lower power consumption

Cons:
- More expensive per TB

Typical Uses:
- Databases
- Virtual machines
- Operating systems
- High-performance applications

Typical Latencies:
Device	  Latency
HDD	      5-15 ms
SSD	      <1 ms
NVMe SSD  Microseconds

Interview Question:
Why databases prefer SSDs?
Random I/O performance is dramatically higher.

