Interview Answer:
HDD (Hard Disk Drive) uses spinning magnetic disks to store data, while SSD (Solid-State Drive) uses flash memory with no moving parts. SSDs provide much lower latency and higher IOPS, making them ideal for databases and virtualized workloads. HDDs remain cost-effective for large-capacity and archival storage.

NetApp Context:
A common NetApp architecture is:

Hot Data     -> SSD
Warm Data    -> SSD/Hybrid
Cold Data    -> HDD or Object Storage


This is called tiered storage, where frequently accessed data stays on fast SSDs and infrequently accessed data moves to cheaper HDDs or object storage.

