Interview One-Liner:
An NVMe SSD is a Solid-State Drive that uses the NVMe protocol over PCIe instead of SATA, providing significantly lower latency, higher IOPS, and much higher throughput than traditional SSDs. For high-performance workloads such as databases, virtualization, and AI systems, NVMe is typically the preferred storage medium.


SATA stands for Serial Advanced Technology Attachment: 
It is an interface (connection standard) used to connect storage devices such as HDDs and SSDs to a computer.

Think of SATA as the road between the computer and the storage device.

Simple Analogy

Imagine:

HDD/SSD = Car
SATA = Road
Data = Passengers

Even if you buy a very fast car (SSD), it can only travel as fast as the road allows.

That's why SATA SSDs are much faster than HDDs, but slower than NVMe SSDs.

